# Settings with a meaning only under another setting, and their behaviour
# without it. There are two answers, because there are two types of
# dependency here, and they are two different questions.
#
# A row that depends on a *switch* becomes grey and keeps its position. The
# setting belongs to the function that the switch turns on. The grey row
# therefore says "turn that function on to use this". The user can still see
# the row and its position, and that is the method to learn that the function
# has this setting.
#
# A row that depends on a *choice* goes away. It does not belong to a function
# that the user turned off. It belongs to one of the effects that one slot can
# hold, and the settings of the other effects are not relevant. Three grey
# temperature marks below a load gauge are three rows about an effect that is
# not on the bar. A switch is different: the choice that brings the rows back
# is the row directly above them, so the rows are not hidden from a user who
# looks for them. See _hides.
#
# An entry is a key, which must be on, or (key, value), which must have
# exactly that value. The temperature settings have a meaning only when an
# effect shows the temperature.
#
# The value of an entry can also be more than one value, and then any one of
# them is sufficient. A desktop scene has a colour under three of the nine
# scenes, and DEPENDS_ON cannot give that as three separate rules. The scenes
# are written here and are not taken from the service, because the tests read
# this table directly. Those tests find a value that is not a scene.
#
# The *key* of an entry can also be more than one key, and then any one of
# them with that value is sufficient. Two settings can select the temperature
# gauge: the rainbow slot in Game Mode, and the scene in Desktop Mode. Its
# marks apply when one of the two selects it. One rule for each setting gives
# two rules that must both be true, which is the opposite meaning. One rule
